            <ArticleTitle>Neuron transplantation partially reverses an obesity disorder in mice.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>133-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.cmet.2012.01.011</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Mice lacking leptin receptors are grossly obese and diabetic, in part due to dysfunction in brain circuits important for energy homeostasis. Transplantation of leptin receptor-expressing hypothalamic progenitor neurons into the brains of leptin receptor deficient mice led to integration into neural circuits, reduced obesity, and normalized circulating glucose levels.</AbstractText>
